<body>
  <GameNav />
  <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a> &gt; <a href="/rooms-and-exits/">Rooms &amp; Exits</a> &gt; Chapter 3 &gt; Level 2</nav>

  <main class="seo-section">
    <h1>Rooms &amp; Exits Chapter 3 Level 2 Walkthrough: Living Room</h1>

    <p>The Living Room is room 2 of Chapter 3, <i>Murder Dressed in White</i>, and it is the odd one out in the chapter: there is <b>no number code</b> in it. No keypad, no dial, no drawer with four digits. Instead you get a 3x3 sliding-tile puzzle set into a box lid, a shelf of labelled bottles, a decoder ring, and a key that opens a door. This page walks the whole 5:58 run in order, marks every puzzle with its timestamp, and is explicit up front about the two things people waste the most time on: the code that does not exist, and the note that cannot be read.</p>

    <div class="disclaimer">Unofficial fan-made guide. Not affiliated with Webelinx Games, the developer of Rooms &amp; Exits: Escape Room Games.</div>

    <div class="verify-note">Built from one 5:58 recording, one frame per second (358 frames). Every written character on this page was cross-checked with macOS Vision OCR across <b>all 358 frames</b> and then confirmed on full-size frames, so names like <i>Impressionism</i>, <i>NCE DE DAMPIERRE</i>, <i>CARA</i>, <i>ANTARES</i> and <i>Ring with numbers</i> are transcribed, not guessed. The two exceptions — the scribbled note and the fifth bottle label — are called out as unreadable rather than filled in.</div>

    <LevelSelector chapter={3} current={2} variant="jump" label="Chapter 3 · Murder Dressed in White" />

    <div class="answer-box">
      <span class="label">Quick answer</span>
      <p>Living Room is room 2 of 15 in Chapter 3, marked <b>Hard</b>. There is no numeric code in this level. Two things finish it:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>The <b>3x3 tile lid</b> on the wooden box on the tall bookshelf is a sliding-tile puzzle. Solve it (in the run: 1:50 to 2:03) and the box opens on a <b>Key</b>.</li>
        <li>Use that <b>Key</b> on the <b>white door on the left wall</b> at 5:53. The level completes at 5:57 with <b>Reward +50</b> and <b>Time: 10:29</b>.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Nothing else in the room is a lock. The bottles, the ring, the blinds and the scribbled note are all either decoration or optional.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="video-wrap">
      <div class="yt-facade" data-yt="9-YCawaFD_0" data-title="Rooms &amp; Exits Level 2 Living Room Walkthrough Murder Dressed in White Chapter 3"
           role="button" tabindex="0" aria-label="Play video: Rooms &amp; Exits Level 2 Living Room Walkthrough">
        <img src="https://i.ytimg.com/vi/9-YCawaFD_0/maxresdefault.jpg"
             alt="Rooms and Exits Chapter 3 Level 2 Living Room walkthrough video" loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1280" height="720"
             onerror="this.onerror=null;this.src='https://i.ytimg.com/vi/9-YCawaFD_0/hqdefault.jpg'">
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="video-cap">The 5:58 run used for every frame on this page.</p>

    <img class="hero" src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l2-hero-living-room.jpg" alt="Rooms and Exits Chapter 3 Level 2 Living Room: mint-green walls, yellow sofa and dark coffee table on a patterned rug, white door and tall bookshelf on the left, fireplace with a gallery wall, console with white drawers and a window with blinds behind" width="1200" height="594" />

    <h2>What this level actually is<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>A modern living room, and a good-looking one: mint-green walls, a dark grey fireplace wall hung with a gallery of small framed prints, dark wood floor under a pale patterned rug, a long yellow sofa, a low dark coffee table, a grey swivel armchair, a tall floor lamp, two white ottomans under the window and a narrow shelf unit of bottles on the right. On the left wall, a plain white door with a tall dark-wood bookshelf beside it.</p>
    <p>Structurally it is the loosest level in the chapter. The chain that actually finishes the room is short — <b>solve the box on the bookshelf, take the key, open the door</b> — and everything else is optional content: five labelled bottles to pick up and read, a ring with digits on it, a sheet of paper scribbled over so heavily that its message is buried, and a set of windows with blinds.</p>
    <p>That is why the level feels longer than it is. The recording spends <b>13 seconds</b> on the box lid and almost <b>two minutes</b> on the scribbled note, and the note is not required. If you only want out of the room, read steps 4, 5 and 9 below and skip the rest.</p>

    <h2>The map: what is where<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>Every object the run interacts with or clearly identifies, and what it is for.</p>
    <table class="item-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Object</th><th>Where</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>White door</td><td>Left wall, beside the bookshelf</td><td>The exit. Lever handle, no keypad, no number. Opened with the Key at 5:53.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Tall bookshelf</td><td>Left wall, beside the door</td><td>Books with real printed titles; the wooden box with the tile lid sits on it.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Wooden box, tiled lid</td><td>On the bookshelf</td><td>3x3 sliding-tile puzzle. Solving it opens the box and yields the <b>Key</b>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Fireplace</td><td>Dark grey accent wall</td><td>White mantel, gallery wall above, shattered glass and logs inside. Nothing to solve.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Console with white drawers</td><td>Back wall, under the window</td><td>Drawers pull open; the run opens and closes them, nothing found.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Window with blinds</td><td>Back wall</td><td>Examined at 1:44. Blinds do not move; nothing behind them.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Yellow sofa / coffee table</td><td>Centre of the room</td><td>The <b>scribbled note</b>, a marble tray of biscuits and grapes, a newspaper.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Bottle shelf</td><td>Right wall</td><td>Five labelled bottles: <b>CARA</b>, <b>ICO</b>, <b>ANTARES</b>, <b>PANIAN</b>, plus one label we could not read.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Ring with numbers</td><td>Carried item, found ~1:08</td><td>Digits around a ring — a decoder. Never used on anything in this run.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Glass of water</td><td>Carried item, seen 1:21</td><td>In the bag with Use / Combine / Dismantle / Examine. No observed use.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Key</td><td>Inside the bookshelf box</td><td>Opens the white door. The only item that ends the level.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Scribbled note</td><td>Coffee table</td><td>Paper covered in dense scribble. Characters show through but are not reliably readable.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Step by step<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>Order follows the recording. Timestamps are video time; the links jump to the matching second.</p>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 1 · 0:00</span>
      <strong>Let the intro clear, then read the room</strong>
      The capture opens on the chapter map — the hex grid of rooms with <i>Living Room</i> marked <b>Hard</b> and <i>Level 3 Study Room</i> still locked — then loads the room. The full view arrives at about 0:12 and the timer starts at 0:00. Sweep it left to right and you have seen everything that matters in five seconds: door and bookshelf left, fireplace and console centre-back, window behind, bottle shelf right, seating in the middle.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l2-hero-living-room.jpg" alt="The Living Room at the start of Chapter 3 Level 2, fully loaded, with the timer at 0:00" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9-YCawaFD_0&t=0s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:00</a></div>
    </div>
    <InContentAd />

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 2 · 0:33 · the bookshelf</span>
      <strong>Read the book spines — then ignore them</strong>
      Zooming the bookshelf gives you real printed spines: <b>Impressionism</b>, <b>NCE DE DAMPIERRE</b> (the tail of a longer French title), <b>ci. Genius is made, not born.</b>, and further along <b>Wiseman</b> / <b>WISEMAN GROUP.</b>, <b>Dreaming of Sweden</b>, <b>The Future that Disappeared</b>. They look like a cipher and they are not: nothing in the level ever refers to a title. What matters on this shelf is the wooden box.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l2-bookshelf-tile-box.jpg" alt="The tall bookshelf in the Living Room with printed book spines and the wooden box with a 3x3 tiled lid" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9-YCawaFD_0&t=33s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:33</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 3 · 1:08 · the ring</span>
      <strong>Pick up the Ring with numbers</strong>
      An item goes into the bag labelled <b>Ring with numbers</b> — a ring with digits printed around it. It is the level's decoder, and if you are the sort of player who wants the note on the coffee table explained, this is the item that probably does it. In this run it is never used, and the level finishes without it, so do not stall here.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l2-ring-with-numbers.jpg" alt="The Ring with numbers item being picked up in the Living Room level" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9-YCawaFD_0&t=68s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1:08</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 4 · 1:50 · the box lid</span>
      <strong>Slide the 3x3 tiles</strong>
      Zoom the box on the bookshelf and the lid is a <b>3x3 grid of nine wooden tiles</b> — light and dark wood in a chequer pattern, one tile fitted with a small knob. That knob is the tile you slide; the empty slot lets the rest move. It is a straight sliding-tile puzzle, not a code, so there is nothing to type and nothing to look up. Restore the pattern and the lid releases.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l2-tile-puzzle.jpg" alt="The 3x3 sliding-tile lid on the wooden box in the Living Room, with one knobbed tile and an empty slot" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9-YCawaFD_0&t=110s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1:50</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 5 · 2:03 · the key</span>
      <strong>The box opens on a Key</strong>
      Thirteen seconds after starting the tiles the box is open, the item name <b>Key</b> appears, and the interior is empty. That is the only thing in the box and the only key in the room. Everything from here to the end of the run is optional.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l2-box-open-key.jpg" alt="The open wooden box on the Living Room bookshelf with the Key inside" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9-YCawaFD_0&t=123s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2:03</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 6 · 2:39 · the bottles</span>
      <strong>Five bottles, four readable labels</strong>
      The narrow shelf on the right wall holds five bottles, each with a printed label. Four read clearly — <b>CARA</b>, <b>ICO</b>, <b>ANTARES</b>, <b>PANIAN</b> — and the fifth reads as something like <b>CHT ALE</b> and stayed unstable across every frame we tried. The player lifts, reads and replaces them for about <b>27 seconds</b>. Nothing opens as a result in this run, so treat this as optional reading rather than a puzzle with a payoff.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l2-bottles-shelf.jpg" alt="The five labelled bottles on the shelf unit in the Living Room" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9-YCawaFD_0&t=159s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2:39</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 7 · 4:00 · the note</span>
      <strong>The scribbled note is the room's time sink</strong>
      On the coffee table, next to a marble tray of dark biscuits and green grapes and a folded newspaper, there is a sheet of white paper covered in dense dark scribble. Characters show through it and the game clearly wants you to decode them — that is what the <b>Ring with numbers</b> is for. We could not read them: at 4x, three separate frames gave three different strings, so this page prints none of them.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l2-scribbled-note.jpg" alt="The scribbled note on the Living Room coffee table beside a marble tray of biscuits and grapes" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9-YCawaFD_0&t=240s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">4:00</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 8 · 1:44 · the window</span>
      <strong>The blinds do not open</strong>
      The back wall window is examined at 1:44 — three panes, horizontal blinds half down, a blurry exterior beyond. Nothing moves, nothing is taped to the glass, nothing is behind the blinds. It is the biggest piece of non-interactive scenery in the room and it wastes a good thirty seconds if you expect it to be a puzzle.
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9-YCawaFD_0&t=104s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1:44</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 9 · 5:53 · the door</span>
      <strong>Use the Key on the white door and you are out</strong>
      Back to the left wall, the white door, the Key from the box. No code is entered — there is nothing on the door to enter it into. The door gives, the room view comes back, and the level closes out.
