    <h2>Where people get stuck<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p><b>"I cannot find the code."</b> There is no code. This is the single most common dead end in the level: Chapter 3 opens with Dining Room, which does have numeric locks, so everyone arrives expecting a keypad. The Living Room finishes on a sliding-tile puzzle and a key. Stop looking for a number.</p>
    <p><b>"The tile lid will not open."</b> It is a sliding puzzle, so treat it as one: only the knobbed tile moves, one step at a time, into the empty slot. Do not tap the tiles expecting them to rotate. In the recording it takes 13 seconds of steady sliding — if you have been on it for five minutes, you are probably trying to make a picture that is not the target; the goal is the chequer pattern the lid already half-shows.</p>
    <p><b>"The key does not fit anything."</b> It fits the white door on the left wall and nothing else. Not the console drawers, not the fireplace, not the bottle shelf.</p>
    <p><b>"The note is unreadable."</b> It is meant to be read through the scribble, and the Ring with numbers is the tool for it. We could not recover the characters either, at 4x, from three different frames — so we are not going to sell you a guess.</p>
    <p><b>"The bottles must be an order puzzle."</b> Maybe, but nothing in this run opens after 27 seconds of handling them, and the level completes without them. If you want to try, the four legible labels are CARA, ICO, ANTARES and PANIAN.</p>
    <p><b>"The blinds and the drawers are hiding something."</b> Neither hides anything in this footage. The drawers open, are looked into, and are closed again.</p>

    <h2>Where Living Room sits in Chapter 3<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>Chapter 3 is <i>Murder Dressed in White</i>. Living Room is room <b>2 of 15</b>, after Dining Room and before Study Room, and it is labelled <b>Hard</b> on the chapter map. The map screen also shows <i>Level 3 Study Room</i> sitting behind an <b>Unlock</b> button while you are in Level 2.</p>
    <p>Its shape is worth knowing before you go in: it is the chapter's item-hunting level. Where Level 1 is numeric locks and Level 4 is a single eight-digit safe, Level 2 makes you find and combine things — a tile lid, a decoder ring, a key — and punishes you for assuming every room in the chapter follows the same rule. If you are working through in order, take that as the hint: check whether a room has a keypad at all before you go hunting for digits.</p>

